* text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.jsonl text eol=lf

# Round-trip fixtures are byte-exact inputs, so Git must not touch them. Last
# rule wins, which is why this sits at the end: the `* text=auto eol=lf` above
# would otherwise normalize the CRLF fixture on checkout — and on Windows CI,
# where the runner sets core.autocrlf=true, it is the only thing standing
# between the corpus and a rewritten line ending.
tests/corpus/** -text

# The plain-text corpus needs the same protection and more of it. Its fixtures
# are not all UTF-8 — one is Windows Big5, two are UTF-16 — and `text=auto`
# guesses per file, so a fixture git decides is text has its terminators
# rewritten on commit, on every platform, not only on Windows checkout. `-text`
# turns the guess off; `-diff` stops git rendering the UTF-16 and Big5 ones as
# mojibake in a diff, which is not correctness but is the difference between a
# reviewable patch and a screen of noise.
tests/corpus-text/** -text -diff
tests/corpus-text-undecodable/** -text -diff
